course: everyday
lesson: 58
title: Strangers and tricky people
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Most strangers are kind, but you cannot tell from someone's looks or friendly smile whether they are safe; a «tricky person» is anyone, stranger or not, who tries to get a child to go somewhere or do something without the parent knowing.
explain: The big rule: never go anywhere with anyone, never get into a car and never take a gift, without checking first with your parent or carer; the point is that the adult who looks after you always knows where you are and who you are with.
explain: Tricky people often ask a child for help («Can you help me find my lost puppy?»), offer sweets, a ride or a gift, or ask you to keep a secret; grown-ups who really need help ask other grown-ups, not children.
explain: Many families choose a secret family password: if someone says «Your mum sent me to pick you up» and does not know the password, you do not go, you tell a trusted adult.
explain: If someone tries to grab you, yell loudly «No! This is not my parent!», kick and pull away, run to a safe place full of people, and tell an adult; if you are lost, the best helpers to ask are a police officer, a shop worker at the counter or a mum or dad with children.
explain: A man at a park in Ohio told a girl he had lost his puppy and asked her to help look in his van; she said no, ran to her mum, and the police were called.
explain: A boy in Leeds was offered sweets and a lift home by a woman he did not know; he got in, and his parents were frightened when he arrived home in a stranger's car, even though he was safe this time.
fact: You cannot tell whether a person is safe just from how they look.
fact: Always check first with your parent or carer before going anywhere with anyone.
fact: Never get into a car with someone unless your parent or carer has said yes first.
fact: Adults who need help should ask other adults, not children.
fact: An «uh-oh» feeling about a person is a warning worth listening to.
fact: A family password helps a child check whether a parent really sent someone.
fact: Stay more than an arm's length away from someone who calls you over to their car.
fact: If someone tries to grab you, yell, pull away, run to people and tell.
fact: Shouting «This is not my parent!» tells everyone nearby that you need help, while people may think a child who only screams is just having a tantrum, a crying fit.
fact: If you are lost, a police officer, a shop worker or a parent with children are good people to ask.
fact: It is fine to be rude, shout or run away to keep yourself safe.
fact: You should tell a trusted adult about anyone who makes you feel uncomfortable.
fact: Tricky people may ask children for help, such as finding a lost pet, because children are kind and want to help; they may also offer treats or say a parent sent them.
fact: Going anywhere with someone without your parent knowing can put you in serious danger.
exercise: A friendly woman at the park says she has puppies in her car and asks you to come and see. What do you do? | Say no, move away and tell your parent | Go quickly, then come straight back before anyone misses you | Go if she seems nice and smiles | Go only if a friend comes with you
exercise: Why can you not tell if someone is safe from their looks? | Dangerous people can look and act friendly | Safe people always wear a uniform or a badge | Dangerous people always look scary to children | You can tell easily if you look at their eyes
exercise: A man you do not know says «Your dad sent me to take you home» but does not know your family password. What do you do? | Do not go, and go to tell a teacher or another trusted adult | Go with him, because he knows your dad's name and where you live | Ask him to say the password again later | Go with him if he has a nice car
exercise: A grown-up asks you to help carry boxes to his van. What is the problem? | Grown-ups who need help should ask other grown-ups | Boxes are always far too heavy for children to carry | Helping adults is always wrong | There is no problem if he says please
exercise: A neighbour you know offers you a ride home from school in the rain. What should you do? | Check first with your parent before getting in | Get in, because you know the neighbour | Get in only if it is raining hard | Get in if the neighbour promises to tell your parent
exercise: A car slows down next to you and the driver calls you over to ask the way. What is safe? | Stay back out of reach and walk away | Go right up to the window to help him | Get in the car so you can show him the way | Lean into the car to see the map he is holding
exercise: Someone grabs your arm in a car park and tries to pull you away. What do you do? | Yell «No! This is not my dad!», pull away and run to people | Stay quiet so they do not get angry | Go with them and try to escape later | Politely ask them to let go
exercise: Why yell «This is not my mum!» instead of just screaming? | People might think a screaming child is just having a tantrum | It is quieter than screaming | It is a rule that must be said | Screaming is always ignored by everyone, so words are the only thing that works
exercise: You get lost in a big shop in New York. Who is the best person to ask? | A worker at the checkout | A man waiting by the exit | Any teenager looking at a phone | A person out in the car park
exercise: What is a family password for? | To check that someone was really sent by your parent | To open the front door of your house when you get home | To log in to your games online | To tell your friends at school
exercise: Someone you have just met gives you sweets and says «Don't tell your mum». Why is this a warning sign? | Safe adults do not ask children to keep secrets from parents | Sweets are always bad for you | It is rude to share sweets | There is nothing wrong if the sweets are wrapped
exercise: Is it all right to be rude to an adult to keep yourself safe? | Yes, your safety matters more than being polite | No, you must always be polite to adults, whatever they do | Only if the adult is a stranger | Only if your parent is watching
exercise: A coach says he will drive you to a match but your mum does not know. What do you do? | Check with your mum first before going | Go, because coaches are always trusted adults | Go and tell your mum when the match is over | Go if other children are in the car too
exercise: You have an uh-oh feeling about a man watching the playground. What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult about it | Ignore it, feelings are not important | Go and ask the man what he wants | Hide alone in the bushes till he goes
test: A boy in Phoenix is playing in the front yard. A woman stops her car and says his mum had an accident and she will take him to the hospital. What should he do? | Run inside and tell an adult at home, not get in the car | Get in fast because his mum needs him | Ask her which hospital, then get in | Get in only if she knows his mum's name and what his mum looks like
test: Which is the best explanation of the «check first» rule? | Your parent should always know where you are and who you are with | Children may never go anywhere at all on their own | You only need to check with strangers | You only need to check if it is getting dark outside
test: A girl in Edinburgh is lost at a train station. Which person should she ask for help? | A station worker in uniform or a mum with children | A man who has been following her around for a while | Someone who offers to take her outside | Nobody, she should leave the station alone
test: A tricky person asks for help finding a lost kitten. Why is this a trick that often works? | Children are kind and want to help animals | Kittens are easy to find anywhere | Children always know where kittens are | It is not a trick, kittens get lost all the time
test: A man at the swimming pool says «If you come with me I'll give you a new game, but it's our secret». Which TWO warning signs does this show? | A gift and a secret from your parents | A swimming pool and a game | Being friendly and being an adult | Speaking quietly and wearing swimwear
test: What would most likely happen if a child shouted «Help! This is not my dad!» in a busy shop? | People nearby would notice and come to help | Everyone would ignore it as a joke | The child would be in trouble for shouting | Nobody would understand what was wrong
test: Which is true? | Most strangers are kind, but you still check first before going with anyone | All strangers are dangerous and must never be spoken to, even when you are lost or hurt | People you know are always safe to go with | Only men can be tricky people
test: Your friend's older brother offers to take you both to the shop. Your mum does not know. What should you do? | Ask your mum first and only go if she says yes | Go, because he is your friend's big brother | Go, because you will be back soon | Go if your friend is going too
test: A friendly man in a park keeps asking a girl where she lives and which school she goes to. What should she do? | Not tell him, move away and tell a trusted adult | Tell him, because he seems interested | Tell him the school but not the name of her street | Ask for his address in return
test: guy in a car says ur mum sent him, get in quick. he looks nice | Do not get in, check with your parent first | Get in, he looks friendly enough | Get in if he knows your name | Get in if he seems in a hurry
test: lady asks me help find her lost puppy round the back. help her? | No, adults should ask other adults for help | Yes, helping lost puppies is kind | Yes, if she looks like a nice lady with a kind face | Yes, but only for a few minutes
test: someones grabbing my arm at the park what do i do | Yell, pull away, run to people and tell | Stay quiet so you do not upset him or make him cross | Go with him and tell later | Wait politely until he lets go
test: he called me over to his car window, said its ok im a teacher | Stay more than an arm's length away and go | Walk right up to hear him better | Go over because teachers are always safe people | Lean in the window to be polite
test: man says mum sent him to pick me up but i don't know him | Do not go, go back inside and check with an adult | Go, he knows your mum's name, so she must have sent him | Go if he has a nice car | Go if it is raining
test: lady wants me to help find her lost dog round the corner | Say no and tell a trusted adult | Help her, because it is kind to help | Go if you are quick and come straight back | Go if she shows a photo of the dog
test: Ella says strangers who smile are always safe. true? | No, a smile does not show if someone is safe | Yes, bad people never smile | Yes, smiles mean kindness | Yes, unless they are sitting in a car and want you to come over
test: heard a man in a white van was asking kids to help find his puppy outside school, everyone's saying he's a kidnapper | Either way, adults who need help ask adults, so say no and tell a grown-up | It proves all white vans are dangerous | It means helping with lost puppies is always fine, whoever asks you to help | It is gossip, so nothing needs doing
test: my mate says u can tell a bad stranger cos they look scary and messy | Wrong; you cannot tell whether a person is safe just from how they look | He is right, bad people look scary | He is right, nice clothes mean safe | He is right, if they are wearing sunglasses and a hat pulled down low over their face
test: someone's saying the new neighbour is a creep cos he's always alone, pass it on | Do not pass it on; being alone proves nothing, and the rumour could hurt him | Pass it on, so everyone keeps away | Pass it on, because living alone is strange | Pass it on, and add that he watches kids from his window all day long and never goes out
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the purpose of a family password? | It enables a child to verify whether a parent truly sent another person | It allows the child to unlock the family car | It is required to enter the school building | It is used to log in to video games and other accounts belonging to the family
exam: It being given that a person in a car calls a child towards the vehicle, select the conduct consistent with established guidance. | To remain more than an arm's length away and not approach | To approach so as to hear the person clearly and give directions | To get in if the person seems kind | To lean into the car window to help
exam: Identify the recommended response where a person attempts to seize a child. | To yell, pull away, run towards people and tell | To remain silent so as not to anger the person | To go quietly and wait for a chance later | To hide behind the person's car
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance concerning travelling with another person. | A child should always check first with a parent or carer before going anywhere | A child may go with any adult who knows his name | A child may go with any adult in uniform | A child may go if the adult offers a reward and promises to bring him back within the hour
